f 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498482 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498482 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186498352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498482 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f6c40a60-2f1f-596b-9290-8a1e85cf9a1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498535 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498535 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337117" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498535 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498535 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e2/e22c8502d1949e3698da9ebd630d746c74be0c8d590e5bbfa1096d9a3cf5e1dc.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\n$q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498535 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "509105eb-35a0-5320-a66f-4c8711db0b98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498535 #edb/ref 1074 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=50\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=50I=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+500q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{4}{25}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+10{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+10\\alpha =\\frac{4}{25}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{2}{125}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{125}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{2}{125}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{100},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{100} \\\\ A{e}^{-10(0)}+\\frac{2}{125} & =\\frac{1}{100} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{3}{500}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(t) & =-\\frac{3}{500}{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{2}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{500}(8-3{e}^{-10t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=-\\frac{3}{500}{e}^{-10t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{2}{125}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498539 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498539 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337118" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498539 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498539 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/69/693b38a5c1e5e07fc2791b3d7a471eae47382326a771c1e6ebc49d3cbd167c16.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\n$q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498539 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "585553db-a353-5891-ba13-8ab633961f14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498539 #edb/ref 1074 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=60\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=60I=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.003}=\\frac{1000}{3}q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n18=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1000}{3}q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}q=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-50t/9},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{50}{9}\\alpha =\\frac{3}{10}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{27}{500}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{27}{500}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-50t/9}+\\frac{27}{500}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{20},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{20} \\\\ A{e}^{-50(0)/9}+\\frac{27}{500} & =\\frac{1}{20} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{250}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(t) & =-\\frac{1}{250}{e}^{-50t/9}+\\frac{27}{500} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{500}(27-2{e}^{-50t/9})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=-\\frac{1}{250}{e}^{-50t/9}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{27}{500}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498556 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498556 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337139" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498556 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498556 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/94/94befb5500eff8908ded191ea1bd018347718823c34f9fbf369791d37708c72c.png)\n\nConsider the RC circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-t}+\\frac{P}{425}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{Q}{425}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498556 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "82951619-49dc-5358-8bd0-2dda9a341d9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498556 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $200\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.005\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=200\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=200I=200\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=200\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+q=\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +\\beta =\\frac{1}{25}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ \\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{425},\\beta =\\frac{1}{425}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{425}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{1}{425}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}-\\frac{4}{425}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{1}{425}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-4,Q=1,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-4+1=-3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498586 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498586 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498586 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498586 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8d/8d928bacb5d981adffa5259c85c828e3be6610f0856085a18f7a090936e6f4d5.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498586 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d7fb2c27-893c-5acc-87d5-bfee51ee7608" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498586 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $100\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.001\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=100\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=100I=100\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.001}=1000q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=100\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+1000q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498598 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498598 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337052" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498598 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498598 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d5/d5be79e0c9f09b5810459bfd8a1ce88f21e2e709dff9d20d7369681ddb61a81b.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498598 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1b0e27c-532b-588c-9b00-39b1140a6bd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498598 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $100\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.005\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=100\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=100I=100\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20=100\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498599 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498599 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337110" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498599 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498599 #edb/ref 1071 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498599 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1d39a1a-0d4c-51d0-9b27-2b99bcd83d50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498599 #edb/ref 1074 "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+8{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha =\\frac{3}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{3}{32}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{32}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{3}{32}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{3}{32}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{32}$ coulombs." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
